WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.741
A practical integer safety baseline for C
starts with knowing the type, range,

2
00:00:04.821 --> 00:00:07.520
and signedness of each security-relevant
value.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.649
Numeric values should have a clear
meaning: length, count, index, offset,

4
00:00:11.729 --> 00:00:13.785
quota, limit, identifier, or status.

5
00:00:13.865 --> 00:00:15.279
Once the meaning is clear,

6
00:00:15.359 --> 00:00:19.536
the allowed range can be checked before
the value controls program behavior.

7
00:00:19.616 --> 00:00:21.544
Validate numeric input before use.

8
00:00:21.624 --> 00:00:25.479
Avoid signed overflow by checking operands
before signed arithmetic.

9
00:00:25.559 --> 00:00:28.901
Detect unsigned wraparound when wraparound
is not intended.

10
00:00:28.981 --> 00:00:33.222
Check conversions before storing into
narrower or differently signed types,

11
00:00:33.302 --> 00:00:37.543
and remember that a cast does not prove
the value is valid for the destination or

12
00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:38.652
the business rule.

13
00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:41.752
Check multiplication and addition before
allocation.

14
00:00:41.832 --> 00:00:45.109
Bound indexes, offsets, loop counters, and
parser lengths.

15
00:00:45.189 --> 00:00:48.402
Validate shift counts, divisors, remainder
assumptions,

16
00:00:48.482 --> 00:00:50.410
and special arithmetic edge cases.

17
00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.024
When arithmetic controls memory, access,
quotas, or resources,

18
00:00:54.104 --> 00:00:58.795
choose clear failure behavior and avoid
trusting a result whose representability was

19
00:00:58.875 --> 00:00:59.968
never established.

20
00:01:00.048 --> 00:01:02.169
Finally, use tooling, boundary tests,

21
00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:05.269
and systematic review as a normal
engineering routine.

22
00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:09.204
Compiler warnings, static analysis,
sanitizers, defensive fuzzing,

23
00:01:09.284 --> 00:01:11.726
and code review all catch different
defects.

24
00:01:11.806 --> 00:01:15.983
The mature habit is to trace numeric data
from input to final use and make every

25
00:01:16.063 --> 00:01:20.754
range, conversion, and arithmetic decision
visible enough for the next maintainer to

26
00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:21.220
trust.
